**Dual-write (phase 1).** Legacy `pg-orders-1` stays the system of record and serves every read. Each write also lands in an `orders_outbox` row inside the same legacy transaction, and `outbox-relay` copies those rows to orders-v2 asynchronously, while `backfill_orders_v2` copies historical orders each night over about 11 nights. Nothing reads from orders-v2 yet. The cutover can't start until four conditions hold:

- the backfill is complete;
- the `order-diff` mismatch rate stays under 0.01% for 3 days;
- relay lag p99 stays under 2 seconds for those same 3 days;
- a one-time full-table checksum matches, which is the only check that covers every old order.

**Cutover (phase 2).** Reads move to orders-v2 first, through `orders.read_from_v2` in daily steps of 1%, 10%, 50% and 100%, followed by a 48-hour soak at 100%. Writes go to both stores the whole time, so rolling back just means setting the flag to 0. Then `orders.write_legacy` is turned off, which makes orders-v2 the only store receiving writes. Legacy stays up read-only for 30 days and is then decommissioned under `CHG-4471`.

The write stop is the point of no return. Nothing syncs from orders-v2 back to legacy, so falling back after the write stop would lose every order written since. That is why it waits for the soak and needs sign-off from the DBA lead and the commerce director.

The timeline has no slack. If any ramp step is held for even one day, the write stop misses 2026-11-30 and slips past the code freeze into January, and dual-write keeps running until then.

